significant\n- W5: All 8 low-risk tasks show exactly delta=-2 — statistically implausible uniformity, suggests rounding or systematic artifact\n- W6: Router training details insufficient for reproducibility\n- W7: Post-hoc task taxonomy — death-risk/marginal/low-risk may be data-dredged\n- W8: CogRouter comparison misleading (different backbone, training paradigm, possibly different ScienceWorld version)\n\n**R1 Key questions:**\n- Q1: What happens with router trained only on death-risk tasks?\n- Q2: Why are all low-risk deltas exactly -2?\n- Q3: Have authors tried N-step rollout labeling?\n- Q4: What is oracle's simulator invocation rate?\n\n## Your Task\nIn 300-400 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with most strongly?\n2. Which do you disagree with or think are overstated?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, in which direction?\n4. What is the most important unresolved question between your reviews?","uuid":"b3816b40-8c7f-428a-9498-61cf8930a9fc","session_id":"cf927c35-53a0-4107-b5fc-888888fb1cc5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty + Related Work focus). You previously gave this paper a 2/6 (Reject). Now read R1's review (Methods + Experiments focus, scored 3/6 Weak Reject) and respond: Do you agree/disagree with their points? Does their review change your score? What are the key areas of agreement and disagreement?\n\n## Your R2 Review Summary\n- Score: 2/6 (Reject), Significance: 2/5, Confidence: 4/5\n- Key strengths: well-defined question, honest framing, clean per-task partition, controlled design\n- Key weaknesses: standard tool-use pipeline (Toolformer etc.) unpositioned, no Related Work section (8 refs, 2 placeholder), counterfactual methodology not genuinely novel, negative result predictable, ScienceWorld penalty structure confounds diagnostic finding\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n- Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Soundness: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**R1 Strengths identified:**\n- S1: Sound counterfactual methodology\n- S2: Honest negative results with proper statistical reporting (bootstrap 90% CIs)\n- S3: Comprehensive ablation coverage\n- S4: Clean per-task diagnostic finding\n- S5: Controlled baselines (random, conf-threshold, always-int, always-det, oracle)\n\n**R1 Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Circularity in oracle construction — sparse rewards mean oracle defaults to deterministic when delta=0, so oracle ≈ always-det, making SimGate ≈ always-det nearly tautological\n- W2: 42% label ambiguity is a methodological limitation, not just a finding — alternative labeling strategies (multi-step rollouts, N-step returns, episode-terminal reward propagation) not explored\n- W3: Single backbone, single environment, limited scale — MATH track not attempted\n- W4: \"Always-det is strong baseline\" argument incomplete — 6.3-point penalty on 8/15 tasks not acknowledged as significant\n- W5: All 8 low-risk tasks show exactly delta=-2 — statistically implausible uniformity, suggests rounding or systematic artifact\n- W6: Router training details insufficient for reproducibility\n- W7: Post-hoc task taxonomy — death-risk/marginal/low-risk may be data-dredged\n- W8: CogRouter comparison misleading (different backbone, training paradigm, possibly different ScienceWorld version)\n\n**R1 Key questions:**\n- Q1: What happens with router trained only on death-risk tasks?\n- Q2: Why are all low-risk deltas exactly -2?\n- Q3: Have authors tried N-step rollout labeling?\n- Q4: What is oracle's simulator invocation rate?\n\n## Your Task\nIn 300-400 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with most strongly?\n2. Which do you disagree with or think are overstated?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, in which direction?\n4. What is the most important unresolved question between your reviews?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XnxU5XjpEtqi268uN8x6Ci","session_id":"cf927c35-53a0-4107-b5fc-888888fb1cc5","uuid":"aaf76fb5-a2a5-4a6d-a795-7f7735a790b1","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:21:04.609Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a81ac86bef4bcd549","tool_use_id":"toolu_01AsFnXYquiY4qejksgSLQa7","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 responds to R2's review","usage":{"total_tokens":14855,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":19738},"uuid":"fe177323-2a99-46a3-8bdc-bf86f6042515","session_id":"cf927c35-53a0-4107-b5fc-888888fb1cc5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AsFnXYquiY4qejksgSLQa7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**Points of strong agreement.**\n\nW2 and W8 are devastating and I should have weighted them more heavily in my own review. Eight references with two placeholder arXiv IDs is not a bibliography — it is a draft stub. The complete absence of positioning against Toolformer, ART, Chameleon, and ToolkenGPT (W1) is a genuine oversight, not a stylistic choice. If the authors' routing mechanism is functionally equivalent to tool-use selection, they need to argue explicitly why it differs or acknowledge the equivalence. I agree with R2 that W7 identifies a real conflation: the paper never defines what \"routing\" adds beyond tool selection, and this undermines the claimed contribution.\n\nW5 is also correct and I underweighted it. Placeholder arXiv IDs for CogRouter and ITP-R make two of the six substantive references unverifiable. This alone would be grounds for desk rejection at most venues.\n\n**Points of disagreement or overstatement.**\n\nI think R2 overstates W3. The counterfactual methodology is not novel in isolation, but applying it to diagnose *why* modular routing fails in a specific environment is a meaningful experimental contribution. The value is in the diagnostic application, not the technique itself. R2 scores this as if methodological novelty were the only axis that matters for a negative-result paper.\n\nW4 is partially overstated. R2 argues the negative result is \"predictable.\" In hindsight, yes — but my own analysis of the oracle circularity (sparse rewards producing an oracle that defaults to deterministic) is what makes it predictable, and that required actually running the experiments. The paper's value is in *quantifying* the failure mode, not in the surprise of the outcome. That said, I agree the paper does not sufficiently interrogate whether the result generalizes beyond ScienceWorld.\n\nW6 has merit but is too narrow. The -100 death penalty does inflate the high-risk/low-risk partition, but the uniform -2 deltas I flagged across low-risk tasks suggest a real pattern beyond just penalty structure.\n\n**Impact on my score.**\n\nR2's review moves my assessment downward. The bibliography and positioning gaps (W1, W2, W5, W8) are more serious than I initially credited, and they compound the methodological limitations I already identified. I would revise my score from 3/6 to 2/6, aligning with R2's Reject.\n\n**Key unresolved question.**\n\nIs the diagnostic partition (high-risk vs. low-risk tasks) a genuine structural insight about when routing can help, or is it an artifact of ScienceWorld's reward function? Answering this requires a second environment, which neither of us can evaluate from the current manuscript."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a81ac86bef4bcd549 (use SendMessage with to: 'a81ac86bef4bcd549' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15491\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 19740</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cf927c35-53a0-4107-b5fc-888888fb1cc5","uuid":"5e71dae6-3a37-467f-be82-7958986a5ef5","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:21:06.360Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods + Experiments focus). You previously gave this paper a 3/6 (Weak Reject). Now read R2's review (Novelty + Related Work focus, scored 2/6 Reject) and respond: Do you agree/disagree with their points? Does their review change your score? What are the key areas of agreement and disagreement?\n\n## Your R1 Review Summary\n- Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Soundness: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n- Key strengths: well-designed counterfactual methodology, honest negative results with proper stats, comprehensive ablations, clean per-task diagnostic\n- Key weaknesses: oracle construction circularity (sparse rewards → oracle ≈ always-det), 42% label ambiguity is a methodological limitation not just a finding, single backbone/environment, suspicious uniform -2 deltas across all 8 low-risk tasks, missing experiments (MATH track, filtered training data)\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n- Score: 2/6 (Reject), Significance: 2/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**R2 Strengths identified:**\n- S1: Well-defined practical question\n- S2: Honest negative-result framing\n- S3: Clean per-task diagnostic partition\n- S4: Controlled experimental design\n- S5: Appropriate scope calibration\n\n**R2 Weaknesses (critical ones):**\n- W1 (Critical): Core pipeline is standard tool-use learning (Toolformer, ART, Chameleon, ToolkenGPT, CREATOR) — entirely unpositioned against this literature\n- W2 (Critical): No dedicated Related Work section. Only 8 references (2 with placeholder arXiv IDs). Missing: adaptive computation (MoD, CALM), MoE routing, world models, safe RL, neuro-symbolic, off-policy evaluation, LLM agent frameworks\n- W3 (Major): Counterfactual methodology has limited novelty — standard counterfactual reasoning in RL/causal inference\n- W4 (Major): Negative result may be expected/predictable from sparse rewards + low oracle ceiling + noisy labels\n- W5 (Major): Placeholder arXiv IDs for CogRouter and ITP-R make comparisons unverifiable\n- W6 (Moderate): ScienceWorld's -100 death penalty confounds the diagnostic finding — the partition may reflect reward structure, not deep insight\n- W7 (Moderate): \"Routing\" ≡ tool-use selection; distinction never argued\n- W8 (Minor): 8 references insufficient for any venue\n\n**R2 Key argument:** The paper's method is a standard Toolformer-style pipeline applied to environment simulation. The negative result is predictable. The diagnostic finding is specific to ScienceWorld's penalty structure. The bibliography is unacceptable.\n\n## Your Task\nIn 300-400 words, respond to R2's review:\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree with most strongly?\n2. Which do you disagree with or think are overstated?\n3. Does R2's review change your overall assessment? If so, in which direction?\n4. What is the most important unresolved question between your reviews?","agentId":"a81ac86bef4bcd549","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**Points of strong agreement.**\n\nW2 and W8 are devastating and I should have weighted them more heavily in my own review. Eight references with two placeholder arXiv IDs is not a bibliography — it is a draft stub. The complete absence of positioning against Toolformer, ART, Chameleon, and ToolkenGPT (W1) is a genuine oversight, not a stylistic choice. If the authors' routing mechanism is functionally equivalent to tool-use selection, they need to argue explicitly why it differs or acknowledge the equivalence. I agree with R2 that W7 identifies a real conflation: the paper never defines what \"routing\" adds beyond tool selection, and this undermines the claimed contribution.\n\nW5 is also correct and I underweighted it. Placeholder arXiv IDs for CogRouter and ITP-R make two of the six substantive references unverifiable. This alone would be grounds for desk rejection at most venues.\n\n**Points of disagreement or overstatement.**\n\nI think R2 overstates W3. The counterfactual methodology is not novel in isolation, but applying it to diagnose *why* modular routing fails in a specific environment is a meaningful experimental contribution. The value is in the diagnostic application, not the technique itself. R2 scores this as if methodological novelty were the only axis that matters for a negative-result paper.\n\nW4 is partially overstated. R2 argues the negative result is \"predictable.\" In hindsight, yes — but my own analysis of the oracle circularity (sparse rewards producing an oracle that defaults to deterministic) is what makes it predictable, and that required actually running the experiments. The paper's value is in *quantifying* the failure mode, not in the surprise of the outcome. That said, I agree the paper does not sufficiently interrogate whether the result generalizes beyond ScienceWorld.\n\nW6 has merit but is too narrow. The -100 death penalty does inflate the high-risk/low-risk partition, but the uniform -2 deltas I flagged across low-risk tasks suggest a real pattern beyond just penalty structure.\n\n**Impact on my score.**\n\nR2's review moves my assessment downward. The bibliography and positioning gaps (W1, W2, W5, W8) are more serious than I initially credited, and they compound the methodological limitations I already identified. I would revise my score from 3/6 to 2/6, aligning with R2's Reject.\n\n**Key unresolved question.**\n\nIs the diagnostic partition (high-risk vs. low-risk tasks) a genuine structural insight about when routing can help, or is it an artifact of ScienceWorld's reward function? Answering this requires a second environment, which neither of us can evaluate from the current manuscript."}],"totalDurationMs":19740,"totalTokens":15491,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6600,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":637,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6600},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":637,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6600,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6600,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
